<div class="page-header">
  <h1 class="page-header__title">What Happens When You Deploy a Website?</h1>
  <p class="page-header__subtitle">
    A web application does not become publicly available simply because its source code
    exists. Getting it into production can involve dependencies, a build, an application
    process, ports, environment variables, networking, a domain, and HTTPS. HelloDeploy
    organises those steps into one workflow.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="card">
  <div class="card__body">
    <ol class="step-list">
      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">Your project source</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            A deployment starts from your code. HelloDeploy connects to a Git repository — a
            stored history of your project that records every change — and reads the exact
            commit you are deploying, so the same code produces the same deployment every
            time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">Project configuration</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            HelloDeploy inspects the repository and suggests settings for the runtimes it
            recognises. You confirm or correct them: the build command, the start command, the
            port your application listens on, and any environment variables it needs.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">Dependency installation</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Most applications rely on packages written by other people. HelloDeploy installs
            them with <code class="code-inline">npm ci</code>, which needs a
            <code class="code-inline">package-lock.json</code> committed to your repository. If
            your project locks with pnpm or Yarn instead, HelloDeploy tells you before you
            deploy rather than letting the build fail.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">Build</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Many projects need a build before they can run in production — bundling frontend
            assets, compiling TypeScript, or producing a framework's production output. What
            runs here depends on your runtime and your build command.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">Start the application</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            HelloDeploy starts your application with the start command you configured, in an
            isolated container, with your environment variables supplied at startup. Your
            application must listen on the port you configured — that is where HelloDeploy
            expects to find it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">Verify the deployment</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Starting is not the same as working. HelloDeploy requests a path on your running
            application and waits for a successful response before treating the deployment as
            healthy. If nothing answers, the deployment is marked failed and your previous
            version keeps serving traffic.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">Route web traffic</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Your application listens on a port that is private to the server. A reverse
            proxy sits in front of it and forwards incoming web requests to the right
            application, which is how many projects can share one server without colliding.
            Only once the new release is healthy does HelloDeploy point that route at it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">HTTPS</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Traffic is served over HTTPS, so what visitors send and receive is encrypted in
            transit rather than readable by anything between them and the server. Secure
            access depends on the domain being configured correctly.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">Connect a domain</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Every project gets a HelloDeploy address immediately. To use your own domain, DNS
            — the system that tells the internet where a domain should send traffic — has to
            point at HelloDeploy. You prove you own the domain with a TXT record, an
            administrator provisions it, and you add the record HelloDeploy gives you.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="step-list__item">
        <span class="step-list__number" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="step-list__title">Logs and future updates</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-muted">
            Every deployment leaves logs you can read, whether it succeeded or failed. When
            your code changes, redeploy: the same configuration runs again, so publishing an
            update is the same short workflow rather than a fresh setup. Recent healthy
            releases are kept, so you can roll back if an update goes wrong.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</div>

